<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philip of Milly</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1171">1171</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philip of Milly was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> noble of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, who exchanged it in 1161 for the lordship of Oultrejordain with its castles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak</a> and Montréal. He became Grand Master of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> in 1169 and died in 1171. Oultrejordain passed through his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephanie-of-milly">Stephanie of Milly</a> to her husbands, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raynald-of-chatillon">Raynald of Châtillon</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philip-of-milly.jpg" alt="Philip of Milly" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_Philippe_de_Milly.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Philip, also known as Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, was a son of Guy of Milly, who witnessed royal charters in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> between 1108 and 1126 and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> around Nablus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>; the historian Malcolm Barber thinks Guy most likely came from Normandy or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>. His mother, according to the late 13th-century Lignages d'Outremer, was a Flemish noblewoman named Stephanie. Philip inherited his father's estates around Nablus and married Isabella before 1144. He and his brothers are absent from royal charters in the reign of King Fulk, who gave the chief offices to his own men; Philip first appears in one in 1138.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>His career rose after Fulk's death, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melisende-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Melisende</a> ruled. Named lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> by 1144, he was sent that year with Elinand, Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a>, and Manasses of Hierges to relieve Edessa, which Imad ad-Din Zengi took before they arrived. At the council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1148, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-crusade">Second Crusade</a>, he and the other native barons were overruled in the decision to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. He backed Melisende against her son Baldwin III, yet helped Baldwin at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashkelon">Ascalon</a> in 1153 and witnessed his charters from 1155.</p><p>Lord of Oultrejordain from 31 July 1161, he strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-karak">Kerak Castle</a> with a ditch and towers, though the king kept the tolls on caravans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes crossing the lordship. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> in January 1166, perhaps after Isabella's death, and gave them much of Oultrejordain, including the castle of Ahamant. Against the order's decision he joined Amalric's invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1167, and at Bilbeis saved the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-of-ibelin">Hugh of Ibelin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the death of Grand Master Bertrand de Blanchefort, Amalric pressed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Templars</a> to elect Philip in August 1169, regaining their support for the Egyptian war. For unknown reasons he resigned in 1171 and was succeeded by Odo de St Amand. He set out with Amalric as ambassador to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and probably died on 3 April 1171, before reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> counted him among the brave men trained from their earliest years in war. His son Rainier died before him, and his daughters were Helena and Stephanie. He briefly employed the troubadour Peire Bremon lo Tort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philip of Milly” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It passed to <b>Philip of Milly</b> and then, through the heiress Stephanie of Milly, to Raynald of Chatillon in 1176.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-oultrejordain">Lordship of Oultrejordain</a></li></ul></section>
